        "Whatever gets submitted has to be entirely the student's own, checked against similarity software before it goes in, and that's the line every tutor here works within. Acceptable coaching looks like this: naming the exact criterion a draft is falling short on, asking a pointed question that exposes a gap in the logic, working through an entirely separate but comparable example, and confirming sources are cited correctly. Not acceptable, under any circumstance: writing text, heavily reworking a passage, or supplying content meant to be submitted as the student's. A tutor suggesting otherwise should be reported and replaced immediately — a whole diploma, not one grade, is what's actually at stake.",
        "Weather deserves a specific mention rather than a footnote. A storm serious enough to suspend classes for several days in September or October can bump a lab session or a supervisor meeting with almost no warning, and a student who padded their personal timeline ahead of the school's actual cut-off handles that far more calmly than one working right against it. A buffer of two or three weeks built into a self-imposed deadline is a small, deliberate habit that avoids a genuinely stressful last-minute scramble.",
