        "Around Grade 10 or 11, several of the Philippines' more established international schools put a real decision in front of a family: keep going from IGCSE into A Level, following the traditional British sequence, or switch onto the IB Diploma where a school runs both side by side. Day-to-day, a Diploma classroom and an A Level classroom don't feel alike. Committing to A Level usually means picking three or four subjects and pursuing each one deeply, with nothing compulsory sitting outside those subjects. The Diploma instead asks a student to juggle six subjects simultaneously alongside Theory of Knowledge, the long essay and CAS — less depth in any one place, more breadth and structure across the full two years.",
